  "raw": ", which would transfer responsibility for overseeing private preschool programs from state to local authorities through either a depth-of-service review or a statewide Hearings process.\n\nSpecifically, SB 837 would require private schools (most of which are not public charter or independent school projects) to test all staff every year against certain \"evidence-based\" evidence about family size and ages, as well as requiring staff members -- early childhood educators and their supervisors -- to submit state forms that will be considered by the public. Additionally, the test for children under 3 years old is developed exclusively based on federal government research approved under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act -- guidelines created for testing the entire educational system throughout this country.\n\nMost of these hearings are combined with other methods: comparisons between your school's employees with others around the state of California; participation in academic screenings -- both verbal abilities and those used for early math abilities are explored; basic acuity of all students (including those who have certain developmental delays);",
  "serialized": ", which would transfer responsibility for overseeing private preschool programs from state to local authorities through either a depth-of-service review or a statewide Hearings process. Specifically, SB 837 would require private schools (most of which are not public charter or independent school projects) to test all staff every year against certain \"evidence-based\" evidence about family size and ages, as well as requiring staff members -- early childhood educators and their supervisors -- to submit state forms that will be considered by the public. Additionally, the test for children under 3 years old is developed exclusively based on federal government research approved under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act -- guidelines created for testing the entire educational system throughout this country. Most of these hearings are combined with other methods: comparisons between your school's employees with others around the state of California; participation in academic screenings -- both verbal abilities and those used for early math abilities are explored; basic acuity of all students (including those who have certain developmental delays);",
